In order to use this you'd do the following:

Create a new list class:
        KList myList;

When you want to add a variable do the following:
        KItem search;
        search.setName("variableName");
        int found = myList.query(search);

Found will be 0 if the item isn't in the list, 1 otherwise.  To actually
get the item from the list (once you know it was there, a special error
item is returned if it doesn't exist, so query first) do this:
        KItem item = myList.find(search);

The instance "item" will now have a copy of the item from the list.  To
add an item to the list:
        KItem newItem;
        newItem.setName("variableName");
        myList.addItem(newItem);

To remove an item from the list:
        KItem search;
        search.setName("variableName");
        if (myList.query(search))
                myList.removeItem(search);

The list identifies items as being the same based on their name.
Currently the item has another field "string info" which can be used if
you like.  If you want to add additional fields to the KItem class, do so
as normal but make sure they get copyed over by the constructors, etc, as
this is critical to the list functioning, it makes heavy use of copy
constructors and the like.
